course: language
lesson: 42
title: Kipling's word games: «Best Beloved», the 'Stute Fish, «Humph!» and the great grey-green, greasy Limpopo
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Kipling wrote the «Just So Stories» to be read aloud, the way he first told them to his own children at bedtime, so the words are made to be heard: they repeat, they rhyme, they bump together and they play tricks.
explain: He talks straight to the listener, «O Best Beloved», he chops the fronts off long words («'Stute» for «astute»), he lets a child's mistake stand («'satiable curtiosity»), and he loves a run of words that start with the same sound: «the great grey-green, greasy Limpopo River».
explain: He also plays with what words mean. The lazy Camel only ever says «Humph!», so the Djinn gives him a «humph» on his back, «we call it hump now, not to hurt his feelings». And old sayings, like «a leopard cannot change its spots», are turned into stories that pretend to explain them.
explain: When you read a page of Kipling, try reading it out loud: you will hear the tricks, and you can use them in your own stories.
fact: «O Best Beloved» is how Kipling speaks to the child listening; talking straight to the reader like this makes a story feel told, not just written.
fact: A clipped word is a long word with a part cut off: «'Stute» comes from «astute» (clever), «'scruciating» from «excruciating»; the apostrophe shows where letters are missing, just as in «don't» and «it's».
fact: English is full of clipped words we no longer notice: «phone» (telephone), «bus» (omnibus), «fridge» (refrigerator), «flu» (influenza) and «maths» (mathematics).
fact: «'Satiable curtiosity» is baby-talk for «insatiable curiosity», wanting to know things so much you are never satisfied; «curtiosity» is a made-up mistake, and Kipling uses it on purpose to sound like a small child.
fact: Alliteration means several words close together starting with the same sound, as in «great grey-green, greasy»; it makes a phrase easy to remember and fun to say.
fact: A refrain is a line repeated again and again in a story or song, like «Yellow-Dog Dingo, always hungry», which comes back every time the Dingo runs; listeners wait for it and join in.
fact: An onomatopoeia is a word that sounds like the noise it names: the Cat's milk-pot cracks «ffft», and «Humph!» sounds like a grumpy snort.
fact: The Camel's «Humph!» turning into a «hump» is a pun, a joke that uses two words that sound alike; in British English «to have the hump» still means to be sulky.
fact: Kipling's Stone Age names are long descriptions joined by hyphens: Taffimai Metallumai means «Small-person-without-any-manners-who-ought-to-be-spanked», so he shortens it to Taffy.
fact: Kipling uses capital letters to make things grand and important, as with «the Djinn in charge of All Deserts», the way a sign uses capitals for a title.
fact: Old sayings in the book: «a leopard cannot change its spots» (people do not change what they are like), «crocodile tears» (pretend sadness), «a month of Sundays» (a very long time) and «the long and the short of it» (the whole thing in a few words).
fact: «Just so» means «exactly right, word for word»; scientists today call a neat-sounding explanation that nobody has tested «a just-so story».
fact: Some words in the book are British and some American: the sailor's «suspenders» are what British people call «braces», which hold trousers up.
exercise: Who is Kipling speaking to when he says «O Best Beloved»? | The child listening to the story | The Elephant's Child in the story | His wife, who is reading | The King of England
exercise: What is «'Stute» short for? | Astute, meaning clever | Student, meaning a pupil | Stupid, meaning silly | Statue, meaning a stone figure
exercise: What does the apostrophe in «'Stute» show? | That letters have been left out | That the fish owns something | That the word is a name | That the word is being shouted
exercise: Which of these is a clipped word? | Fridge, from refrigerator | Butterfly, from butter and fly | Sunshine, from sun and shine | Elephant, from Greek
exercise: What is «bus» clipped from? | Omnibus | Business | Bustle | Busy
exercise: What does «'satiable curtiosity» mean? | Always wanting to know more | Being hungry for bananas | Fear of crocodiles | Being too shy to ask
exercise: What is alliteration? | Words close together that start with the same sound | Words at the ends of lines that rhyme together | A word that sounds like a noise | A joke with two meanings
exercise: Which phrase uses alliteration? | Great grey-green, greasy | Best Beloved, listen now to me | Once upon a time | The Cat who walked by himself
exercise: What is a refrain? | A line repeated again and again | A word with a part cut off the front | A long name joined by hyphens | A word borrowed from another language
exercise: What is onomatopoeia? | A word that sounds like the noise it names | A word made by joining two short words together | A word that means its opposite | A word that is spelled backwards
exercise: Why does the Camel get a «humph» on his back? | He keeps saying «Humph!», so the Djinn plays on the word | He ate too many thorns and prickles in the desert | The Dog, the Horse and the Ox put it there to punish him for laziness | He carried a heavy load and it got stuck
exercise: What is a pun? | A joke that uses words that sound alike | A story about animals that talk | A very long word made of several short words | A line repeated many times
exercise: What does «to have the hump» mean in British English? | To be sulky and grumpy | To have a bad back after work | To be very lazy | To be very thirsty
exercise: What does Taffy's long name really mean? | Small-person-without-any-manners-who-ought-to-be-spanked | Girl-who-draws-the-best-pictures | Daughter-of-the-great-chief-of-the-whole-tribe-by-the-river | Child-who-asks-a-very-many-questions-all-day-and-all-night-long
exercise: What are «crocodile tears»? | Pretend sadness | Tears of real joy | Tears from cutting onions | Salt water in rivers
exercise: How long is «a month of Sundays»? | A very long time | Exactly four weeks | Only one day a week | A short holiday
exercise: What do British people call the sailor's «suspenders»? | Braces | Belts | Socks | Laces
test: «why does he keep saying the same Dingo line, is he lazy or what» What is the better answer? | It is a refrain: listeners wait for it and join in | He forgot that he had already written it on the page before | The printer copied the same line by mistake | It is a spell that makes the Dingo run
test: Mia writes «the silly, slippery, slithering snake». Which of Kipling's tricks is she using? | Alliteration | A refrain | A clipped word | A pun
test: ★★ Which word is NOT a clipped word? | Butterfly | Phone | Flu | Maths
test: «Kipling can't spell, 'curtiosity' isn't even a word» What would you say? | He made the mistake on purpose to sound like a little child | He really could not spell | It was the right spelling in 1902 | It is the American spelling of curiosity
test: A comic has «CRASH!», «BANG!» and «WHOOSH!». What are these words called? | Onomatopoeia | Alliteration | Refrains | Puns
test: Grandad grumbles «don't mind him, he's got the hump». What does he mean? | He is in a sulky mood | He has a bad back after gardening | He has turned into a camel | He has eaten too much
test: ★★ Your friend says «a leopard can't change its spots» about a boy who keeps cheating. What does she mean? | People don't easily change what they are really like | Leopards really can change their spots if they try | The boy has spots and needs a doctor | The boy likes leopards
test: «bruv he cried crocodile tears when he lost the game, proper actor» What does that mean? | He pretended to be sad | He cried so much he made a river | He was really, truly heartbroken | He laughed until he cried
test: ★ Kipling's Stone Age names describe the person, like Tegumai's «Man-who-does-not-put-his-foot-forward-in-a-hurry». What would a Stone Age name for a boy who is always hungry look like? | Boy-who-is-always-hungry | Hungry Boy Smith | Mr Always | Boy 3
test: A scientist calls an idea «a just-so story». What does she mean? | It sounds neat but nobody has tested it | It has been tested many times and is certainly true | It was written by Kipling himself | It is a bedtime story for babies
test: ★★★ Kipling writes «the Djinn in charge of All Deserts» with capitals. What does that do? | It makes the title sound grand and important | It shows that the words are being shouted loudly | It shows where a new sentence begins | It is a mistake by the printer
test: «'Stute» drops letters from the front of the word. Which of these drops letters from the front too? | Bus, from omnibus | Exam, from examination | Gym, from gymnasium | Maths, from mathematics
exam: Identify the device employed in the phrase «the great grey-green, greasy Limpopo River». | Alliteration, the repetition of an initial sound in neighbouring words | Onomatopoeia, the imitation of a sound by a word | A refrain, the repetition of an entire line | A pun, the exploitation of words of similar sound
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the word «'Stute»? | It is a clipped form of «astute», the apostrophe marking omitted letters | It is a word of the Tegumai language, signifying «a small fish of the sea» | It is the American spelling of «student» | It is a contraction of «it is cute»
exam: Select the response consistent with the account given of the Camel's hump. | It is the subject of a pun upon the Camel's habitual utterance «Humph!» | It is explained by the author as a consequence of his diet of thorns | It is described as a reservoir of water | It is attributed to the burdens placed upon him by Man
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression «a month of Sundays». | A very long period of time | A period of exactly four weeks | A month without any working days | A holiday taken on Sundays
